Basically, I emerge the original package with -uvD (deep dependancy =
check)
My default CFLAGS for use with the Compaq Compiler:

(Default gcc and g++)
/etc/make.conf
CFLAGS=3D"-mcpu=3Dev56 -mtune=3Dev56 -O3 -pipe"

I then switch to Compaq CC with:
CC=3Dccc
CXX=3Dcxx
CFLAGS=3D"-fast -tune ev56 -std1"

Note if I want even better performance I will change -tune to -arch.

-std1 means used Strict ANSI

if -std1 fails, I will try with -std (a more relaxed compiler =
compatibility)
man ccc to see what each does.

And if that fails I finally reset the CC and CXX environment variables =
back to gcc and g++ and emerge the one package that failed.

then go back to ccc compiler for the next package in the dependancy tree

BTW I started my system out with stage1-1.4-rc1
I'm currently building another at work and started at stage3-1.4 similar =
hardware (actually the same type of hardware).
Both systems are AlphaServer  800 5/500 ev56 systems.

